  "final_span": "comes with other downsides however. Groups in the draw will be formed from eight pots, differing from the current system that divides nations by region.\nThese teams will also be seeded, with the top eight ranked ones assigned to group A, progressing down to group H.\nThis is where the fun starts. Nations such as Ireland, Scotland, and Wales aren't likely to object to staying in pot one because they gain advantage from the fair draw system\u2014ending up in a group with lesser-known clubs, well-prepared teams, and progressing by outmaneuvering four smaller opponents in two-match series.\nYet, two additional groups of countries await their fate in pot one, relishing the fair distribution during qualifiers but later regretting what they see as an unfair treatment in the finals.\nFrom a personal perspective, I do not have the time to discuss the impact this will have on European teams and their supporters. Not now. However, it might be worthwhile to note what various European journalists remarked during the voting period.\nEuropean Journalists Association president Gianni Merlo - \u201cExpanding the World Cup has the aim of winning over new consumers, yet at this very moment there is no success anywhere in the world in terms of viewing audience.\u201d\nJournalist John Duerden - \u201cGovernments pay billions to FIFA and spend billions on infrastructure, just so that their citizens can watch Europeans play football.\u201d\nNorwegian journalist Geir Gulliksgaard - \"At a time when there is no progress in either the expansion of the continental club game or the global game to enable more countries to play more often, this move appears senseless and self-serving.\"\nFootball Australia's Football Stakeholders Manager, Jay Coates, was joined by Member Federations President Luke Bessey as they observed the FIFA Council meeting in Moscow. Picture: Football Australia/Sydney FBorooters\nThese views are in accordance with FFA\u2019s stance.\nUp until this point, FFA\u2014one of the lone two associations to publicly oppose the change\u2014had advocated for an 80-team tournament featuring the top 24 nations globally along with qualification slots for non-elite countries.\nThere was some focus on the platoons idea",
